<EXPERIMENT_PACKAGE><EXPERIMENT accession="SRX20410905" alias="GSM7372219_r1"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRX20410905</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="GEO">GSM7372219_r1</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS><TITLE>GSM7372219: YQ0037; Homo sapiens; RNA-Seq</TITLE><STUDY_REF accession="SRP438305"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRP438305</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioProject">PRJNA973632</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS></STUDY_REF><DESIGN><DESIGN_DESCRIPTION/><SAMPLE_DESCRIPTOR accession="SRS17723645"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS17723645</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="GEO">GSM7372219</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS></SAMPLE_DESCRIPTOR><LIBRARY_DESCRIPTOR><LIBRARY_NAME>GSM7372219</LIBRARY_NAME><LIBRARY_STRATEGY>RNA-Seq</LIBRARY_STRATEGY><LIBRARY_SOURCE>TRANSCRIPTOMIC</LIBRARY_SOURCE><LIBRARY_SELECTION>cDNA</LIBRARY_SELECTION><LIBRARY_LAYOUT><PAIRED/></LIBRARY_LAYOUT><LIBRARY_CONSTRUCTION_PROTOCOL>Total RNA was extracted from epithelial cells using the TRIzol method according to standard protocols (Life Technologies, Inc., Gaithersburg, MD, USA). The purified products were evaluated using the Agilent 2200 TapeStation and diluted to 10 pM for cluster generation in situ on the HiSeq2500single-end flow cell followed by sequencing (1 × 50 bp) on the HiSeq 2500. rRNAs were removed from total RNA using an EpicentreRibo-Zero rRNA Removal Kit (Illumina, USA). Then, the PCR products were size-selected by polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis according to the instructions of the NEBNext® Multiplex mRNA/LncRNA Library Prep Set for Illumina® and Chain specific library sequencing was performed using the UNG enzymatic method (New England Biolabs, Inc., Ipswich, MA, USA).</LIBRARY_CONSTRUCTION_PROTOCOL></LIBRARY_DESCRIPTOR></DESIGN><PLATFORM><ILLUMINA><INSTRUMENT_MODEL>Illumina HiSeq 2500</INSTRUMENT_MODEL></ILLUMINA></PLATFORM></EXPERIMENT><SUBMISSION lab_name="" center_name="Peking Union Medical College Hospital" accession="SRA1640219" alias="SUB13383481"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRA1640219</PRIMARY_ID><SUBMITTER_ID namespace="Peking Union Medical College Hospital">SUB13383481</SUBMITTER_ID></IDENTIFIERS></SUBMISSION><Organization type="institute"><Name>peking union medical college hospital</Name><Contact email="jinlipumch@hotmail.com"><Address><Street>Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology</Street><City>Beijing</City><Sub>-- None selected --</Sub><Country>China</Country></Address><Name><First>Li</First><Last>Jin</Last></Name></Contact></Organization><STUDY center_name="GEO" alias="GSE232713" accession="SRP438305"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRP438305</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioProject" label="primary">PRJNA973632</EXTERNAL_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="GEO">GSE232713</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS><DESCRIPTOR><STUDY_TITLE>Combined Proteomics and Transcriptomics Identifies Serpin Family C Member 1 Associated Protein as a Biomarker of Endometriosis</STUDY_TITLE><STUDY_TYPE existing_study_type="Transcriptome Analysis"/><STUDY_ABSTRACT>Objective: To explore potential biomarkers indicating endometriosis.   Results: A sum of 979 mRNAs and 39 proteins were tested to be significantly differentially expression in the standard cluster compared with the endometriosis cluster. Venn analysis showed a filtered signature of only two down-regulated molecules in the EM group, i.e., fetuin B and serpin family C member 1; the latter showed a big variance between the control category and the EM set in the authentication test.  Conclusion: Serpin family C member 1 may be a useful possible biomarker for the analysis of EM. Overall design: Materials and methods: A proteomics method and combined quantitative transcriptomics was adopted to highlight markers in the Endometriosis (EM). Venn analysis was used to integrate the ribonucleic acid sequencing (RNA-seq) and protein profiles. Promising candidate markers were tested by enzyme-related immunosorbent assay.</STUDY_ABSTRACT><CENTER_PROJECT_NAME>GSE232713</CENTER_PROJECT_NAME></DESCRIPTOR></STUDY><SAMPLE alias="GSM7372219" access